<article class="env-warning" id="05WJ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05WJ">Warning <span data-tag="05WJ">8.6.8.6</span>.</a> We have now attached two meanings to the notation $\operatorname{Tr}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
If $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an (essentially small) cartesian fibration, then $\operatorname{Tr}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ can denote the contravariant transport representation of $U$, regarded as an object of $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}})$ (Notation <a data-tag="05WH" href="/tag/05WH">8.6.8.5</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an (essentially small) cocartesian fibration, then $\operatorname{Tr}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ can denote the covariant transport representation of $U$, regarded as an object of $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}})$ (Notation <a data-tag="019S" href="/tag/019S">5.6.5.16</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> In the case where $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is both a cartesian and cocartesian fibration, these usages are in conflict. However, in this case there is a simple relationship between the covariant and contravariant transport representations of $U$: see Remark <a data-tag="05WW" href="/tag/05WW">8.6.8.21</a>. </p>
</article>
